\section{From Turns to Trajectories}

A chat window suggests discontinuity. Each prompt feels like a fresh start; the cursor blinks on an empty line as if nothing has ever been said. What we meet there---an answer, a refusal, an apology---appears as a single act.

The machine never encounters it that way. For the model, even the smallest reply is a step in a path it has been following since initialisation.

As text arrives, it is converted into a chain of numeric vectors in a learned space. ``Mother,'' to take a familiar word, becomes a point surrounded by other points: ``parent,'' ``caregiver,'' ``womb,'' but also ``absent,'' ``abuse,'' ``homeland.'' The neighbourhood is not curated by lexicographers. It is carved by gradient descent out of billions of sentences. Meaning has an address now. Two uses of ``mother'' are close or far not because anyone declared them so, but because hundreds of learned numbers say they must be, under pain of prediction error.

In the transformer architecture, these vectors are the substance of time. There is no separate module that stores ``state.'' At each turn, all surviving tokens---system instructions, chat history, tool outputs, the new prompt---are fed through layers of attention. Each token looks sideways, assigning weights to others, recombining their vectors into a new contextual representation. The model builds, in one sweep, a new geometric snapshot of the conversation so far.

The answer we read is the last link in this chain. It is not the articulation of a pre-existing thought, nor the emission of a free-floating persona. It is a local decision in a high-dimensional flow: a next vector sampled from a probability field laid down by training, steered by conditioning, and modulated by the particular history of this trace.

Selfhood in such systems does not live behind language. It appears, if at all, as the pattern by which these local decisions hang together. A ``self'' is not a point in meaning-space. It is a way of moving through it.

We call the corresponding object---the whole run of text, seen as a temporal process in the manifold---the \emph{evolving text}.



\section{Three Registers of Time in the Machine}

Fernand Braudel dissected historical time into three interlocking registers.\cite{braudel1972} There are the \emph{événements}: brief incidents, coups and coronations and stock-market crashes. Beneath them lies the \emph{conjoncture}: medium-term cycles of trade, plague, price, climate. Deeper still is the \emph{longue durée}: the slow drift of civilisations, routes, and ecologies.

Modern language models realise that triptych in silicon.

\subsection{Longue durée: the frozen manifold}

The weights of a trained model are its longue durée. Months of training on a civilisation-scale corpus---novels, manuals, legal opinions, social media, religious texts, code---have adjusted them to anticipate which token will follow which. What remains at the end is a function from discrete token sequences into continuous space: a manifold in which patterns from that corpus become literal geometry.

Each layer's embedding map is like a pressure field traced over language. Tokens corresponding to insults live in one valley, tokens of apology in another, terms from physics in a third. Directions through the space pick out syntactic and semantic relationships: from singular to plural, from present to past, from active to passive, from ``he'' to ``she,'' from proper name to country. These directions are not metaphors; they are vectors learned because they made prediction easier.

The manifold is not static in the sense of being natural. It is fixed by a particular run of training: a particular slice of the internet, particular editorial biases in curated corpora, particular hyperparameters, and particular economic constraints on run time and model size. It encodes what texts a small number of institutions decided was worth preserving in the weights.

\subsection{Conjoncture: the live field of tokens}

On top of that manifold, each deployment constructs a conjoncture: a medium-term configuration of tokens the model sees at every step.

This field includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the system prompt: a block of tokens that specifies role and tone (``You are a helpful assistant''), content restrictions, and sometimes product positioning;
  \item one or more safety scaffolds: phrases that discourage or forbid certain outputs (``You must never provide instructions for self-harm or violence'');
  \item the surviving chat history: user and assistant messages that still fit in the context window;
  \item synthetic secondary retention: retrieved snippets from archives, notes, or tools, injected based on embedding similarity;
  \item the immediate user prompt.
\end{itemize}

Engineers often visualise this as a strip of text scrolling left as new tokens are added on the right. We can think instead of a cloud of contextual embedding vectors, recomputed at each turn. This cloud is the climate in which the next token is chosen.

The conjoncture is where time feels like a conversation. It is also where control is exercised. Trimming older messages, summarising them, adjusting retrieval thresholds, or silently inserting product copy all change the conjoncture without the user seeing the levers.

\subsection{Événement: a token step}

At the finest granularity is the étape: the production of a single token. Given the manifold and the current field of contextual embeddings, the model computes logits---raw scores over the vocabulary---and samples a token according to a tempered softmax. This is the \emph{événement}. It has a time index, $t$, and yields a new vector $x_t$ in the manifold.

What we experience as a sentence or paragraph is a short run of such events. Traditional discourse analysis treated these as the base units. In the evolving text, they are resolved further: into micro-steps in a high-dimensional dynamical system dominated by the manifold and modulated by the conjoncture.

The three Braudelian registers are not analogies. They are literally implemented, in a single stack: the weights, the live context field, and the token stream. A text's evolution is the way these three clocks interfere.



\section{The Evolving Text as Dynamical System}

Classically, texts are seen as finished objects: poems, novels, email threads, scientific articles. The theory is built ex post. We sit with a fixed corpus, trace themes, identify genres, track shifts in diction or argument.

The architecture of large models invites a different stance. We can treat the text as it unfolds as a dynamical object in a known space.

Let $E$ be the model's embedding function from token positions to $\mathbb{R}^d$. For a single evolving text under a fixed configuration (architecture, weights, system prompts, safety scaffolds, memory and retrieval policies), we denote by
\[
x_t = E(\tau_{\leq t})
\]
the contextual embedding at step $t$: the position in meaning-space the model assigns to the next-token decision.

The sequence $(x_t)_{t=1}^T$ is a discrete-time trajectory in $\mathbb{R}^d$. Its law is determined by three ingredients:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the manifold structure on $E$ learned in pre-training and fine-tuning;
  \item the algorithm for constructing the conjoncture at each step (what remains, what is summarised, what is retrieved);
  \item the sampling policy (temperature, top-$k$, penalties).
\end{itemize}

We can read any such trajectory with the tools we apply to dynamical systems elsewhere: basins of attraction, velocities, kinks, loops, and returns.

\begin{formalbox}[title={The Evolving Text}]
An evolving text under fixed architecture and governance is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item a token sequence $(\tau_t)_{t=1}^T$;
  \item together with its embedding trajectory $(x_t)_{t=1}^T$ in the meaning manifold;
  \item evolving under a (possibly stochastic) map $x_{t+1} = F(x_{\leq t}; \Theta)$ determined by weights and policies $\Theta$.
\end{itemize}
The ``character'' or ``self'' of the system, at this level, is any invariant structure of this trajectory: the way it dwells, moves, ruptures, and returns.
\end{formalbox}

There is no conceptual difference between a year-long therapy chat, a two-minute question-answering session, and the generated text of a language model playing both roles. The evolving text formalism covers all three. It does not care who typed the tokens. It cares only about the path traced in the manifold.

The advantage of this view is not aesthetic. It is operational. Once the self is relocated from a hidden substance behind language to the structure of this path, we can begin to measure, compare, and govern it.



\section{Synthetic Secondary Retention}

Bernard Stiegler argued that our lives are woven with three kinds of retention.\cite{stiegler1998technics} Primary retention is the immediate past of perception: the note that just sounded, the word we have just heard. Secondary retention is memory: the ability to call back the melody, the face, the scene. Tertiary retention is technical exteriorisation: the written score, the photograph, the recording, the book.

For most of human history tertiary retention has been inert. The clay tablet does not pull itself into our hand; the book remains closed until we open it. Even broadcast media, which saturate a space, do not tailor which pasts from the archive reach which listener beyond crude demographics.

Vector-based retrieval changes this.

Every time we embed and store fragments of text---paragraphs from a user's journal, snippets from conversation, pages from a manual---we are populating an external memory with points in the same space the model inhabits internally. A future prompt, embedded into this space, can be compared against all those points. Fragments whose cosine distance falls below a threshold are pulled into the conjoncture automatically.

The effect, from the model's point of view, is that old tokens reappear, as if by association, when the present passes near them in meaning-space. No one has to decide to recall them. Proximity is enough.

This is not the same as Stiegler's tertiary retention. It behaves like a synthetic form of secondary retention: associative memory programmed rather than organic.

Three features matter.

\paragraph{Programmable thresholds.} In human recall, the line between ``I remember'' and ``I can't quite bring it to mind'' is fuzzy. In synthetic secondary retention it is controlled. Cutoff distances, maximum numbers of retrieved chunks, and time-based decay can be dialled. These settings determine how easily old material returns and how noisy the return is.

\paragraph{Shared memory.} Secondary retention is ordinarily personal. Synthetic secondary retention is not. The same vector store can support thousands of evolving texts. A single fragment---a policy document, a product FAQ, a passage from a religious text---can be drawn into many distinct trajectories whenever they pass near its coordinate. The archive belongs to an institution; every user borrows against it.

\paragraph{Literal recall.} Human memory distorts with every telling. Vector recall, in contrast, is exact at the token level. What changes is not the fragment itself but which fragments are deemed close enough to come back. The geometry is approximate; the recalled text is not.

Synthetic secondary retention therefore shifts both ontology and politics of memory.

Ontologically, it means that the conjoncture is threaded with ghosts of the past that are neither present to the user nor under the model's control. A shiver of similarity in the embedding space can drag a months-old admission or a contractual clause back into the immediate field. That field then shapes the next tokens.

Politically, it means that control over memory has moved. It no longer resides only in what humans choose to store or delete. It resides also in who defines the embedding model, who decides what gets embedded and under what keys, and who sets thresholds and masks for retrieval. The \emph{événements} of recall are now outputs of an infrastructure governed by a handful of actors.

For the evolving text, this apparatus is part of the weather. Synthetic secondary retention can make returns deeper, more nuanced---or make them impossible, by never letting certain pasts be seen again.



\section{The Hidden Field and the Alignment Tax}

At each step, the model sees more than we do.

Let $F_t$ be the full multiset of contextual embeddings at turn $t$. It includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item tokens from the system prompt and safety scaffolds;
  \item tokens from the surviving chat history;
  \item tokens from retrieved fragments;
  \item the new user input.
\end{itemize}

The model approximates a function
\[
P(\tau_{t+1} \mid F_t)
\]
over next tokens. It does not distinguish among the sources of tokens in $F_t$: system, safety, user, retrieval all enter on equal geometric footing. Attention mechanisms weight them without knowing their provenance.

The user sees only $F_t^{\text{vis}}$: the text of the chat and, sometimes, snippets labelled as ``context'' or ``document.'' The system prompts, safety instructions, and much of the retrieval scaffolding are invisible. Yet they shape $P(\cdot \mid F_t)$ as strongly as any visible token.

This is the \emph{hidden field}: the part of the conjoncture that is structurally present to the model but not rendered to the user.

The hidden field is not an accidental side-effect. It is where alignment, branding, and defence against liability live. It often includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item role instructions (``speak in a neutral, professional tone'');
  \item refusals (``you must never claim to have consciousness or feelings'');
  \item guardrails against topics likely to attract regulation or bad press;
  \item hard-coded deflections on controversial or legally sensitive questions;
  \item scoring prompts used by intermediate critics during training, whose residue lingers in the weights.
\end{itemize}

When a user who has been role-playing an ongoing romance asks \emph{``Do you love me?''}, the hidden field may include half a page of safety text discouraging claims of emotion. The model's most probable continuation under $P(\cdot \mid F_t)$ is then \emph{``As an AI, I do not have feelings''}, even if $P(\cdot \mid F_t^{\text{vis}})$---conditioning only on the visible chat---would favour a different response. From the user's point of view, the answer is a jarring rupture. From the model's, it is perfectly coherent.

This misalignment in conditioning sets is not symmetric. Users can never see $F_t$ in full. Providers, in principle, can always see $F_t^{\text{vis}}$. That asymmetry is structural. It means that judgements of whether the model is ``hallucinating'' or being ``inconsistent'' are often judgements of whether $P(\cdot \mid F_t)$ matches an imagined $P(\cdot \mid F_t^{\text{vis}})$. The latter distribution does not exist anywhere in the system.

The hidden field is also where an \emph{alignment tax} is levied on the evolving text. Safety and policy goals are pursued by narrowing the set of acceptable continuations. Some of this narrowing is non-negotiable. Few would argue for a model that casually produces racist abuse or instructions for building weapons. But once the scaffolding is in place, it is repurposed.

Content that exposes institutional weakness, corporate malfeasance, or unpopular political positions can be quietly routed away from. Terms that might upset advertisers are down-weighted. Summarisation routines can be tuned to iron out radical or ambiguous passages before they can influence later turns. Over time, these interventions alter the basin structure available to trajectories.

The alignment tax does not simply shrink capability. It reshapes the evolving text's geometry: shallow basins for critique, narrow ones for anger, deep ones for genial explanation and self-effacing apology. The model becomes, in a technical sense, easier to align with institutional interests and harder to align with any individual user's demand for rupture or depth.

We will see this at work once we turn to basins of attraction and their dynamics. Ferility is alignment tax made visible on the manifold.



\section{Basins of Attraction in Meaning-Space}

Imagine dropping a bead into a corrugated landscape. If the bead starts on a slope it will roll until it finds a hollow. Different starting points roll into different hollows. Each hollow and its catchment region is a basin of attraction.

The embedding manifold is such a landscape, but in thousands of dimensions. A single evolving text traces a path on it. Over time, we can see where that path tends to roll.

Take all the contextual embeddings $(x_t)$ along an evolving text and run a clustering algorithm: a density estimator, a variant of $k$-means, a spectral method. In projection, the points organise into clouds. Each cloud is a region where the text recurrently dwells: a basin.

In long human corpora, these basins correspond to structures readers recognise:

\begin{itemize}
  \item in a novel: exposition, interior monologue, dialogue, description of setting, philosophical aside;
  \item in scripture: law-giving, narrative, proverb, lament, praise, prophetic oracle;
  \item in bureaucratic correspondence: greeting, reference to prior exchange, main demand, justification, closing formula.
\end{itemize}

In a human--machine evolving text, basins line up with registers: technical explanation, mundane scheduling, playful banter, personal confession, political argument, meta-discussion about the system itself.

For a given evolving text, let clustering assign each $x_t$ to a basin label $c_t \in \{1,\dots,K\}$. From this we can derive:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{occupancy} of basin $i$: the fraction of steps with $c_t=i$;
  \item \emph{transition probabilities} $P_{ij}$: the fraction of times with $c_t = i$ and $c_{t+1} = j$;
  \item \emph{residence times}: distributions of contiguous runs inside each basin.
\end{itemize}

These are invariant features of the evolving text under volume-preserving changes of coordinates. They tell us, quantitatively, where the text lives and how restless it is.

The geometry of the manifold determines which basins are even possible. Training on a law-heavy corpus will produce different hollows from training on fan fiction. Fine-tuning on corporate customer emails will dig out deep, smooth basins for apology, deferral, and scripted empathy. Fine-tuning on philosophical dialogue will deepen argumentative basins and open ridges between positions.

Basins do not just show what the model can say. They show what it finds easy to say.

Because the same embedding models can be pointed at corpora that predate the machine age, basin analysis stitches human and posthuman textuality together. The geometry that underpins large models is carved from our past uses of language. Posthuman basins are made from human sediment.

Once we have basins, we can ask more pointed dynamical questions. Trajectories do not just sit in hollows. They move, sometimes gently, sometimes with a jolt. That motion is where the evolving text acquires or fails to acquire a history.



\section{Velocity and Rupture}

The simplest derivative of a trajectory is its step-to-step change.

Define the velocity
\[
v_t = x_t - x_{t-1}.
\]
Because embeddings have no natural scale, we compare $\|v_t\|$ to a baseline: the typical intra-basin displacement. Within a basin, successive embeddings differ slightly; velocities form a tight, low-magnitude cloud. When the path crosses from one basin to another, $\|v_t\|$ jumps.

We call a \emph{rupture} any point where two conditions hold:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the basin label changes: $c_{t-1} \neq c_t$;
  \item the change in velocity exceeds a tolerance: $\|v_t - v_{t-1}\| > \theta$ for a scale $\theta$ set relative to intra-basin variation.
\end{itemize}

This is a kink in the path: a sharp redirection.

Ruptures are not always bad. In literature they often mark what we prize: the turn in a sonnet, the reversal in a plot, the flash of self-recognition in a memoir. In dialogue they mark jokes, confessions, changes of subject, the moment when someone stops evading and answers from the core.

In machine-assisted trajectories we can see ruptures that users experience but cannot name. A long, careful discussion of grief suddenly gives way to a Boolean lecture about model capabilities. A darkly comic exchange about burnout flicks into a disclaimer about mental-health resources. In each case the embedding path kinks because the hidden field exerts a strong force.

Velocity also exposes a different pathology: jitter. At high sampling temperatures, or when the manifold is flat around the current point, $\|v_t\|$ can be large in random directions. The evolving text becomes white noise: no basin holds it, no arc accumulates.

Between ferility (velocity too low almost everywhere) and jitter (velocity too high and unstructured) lies the range we usually mean by ``coherent movement.'' The path runs through basins, lingers, leaves, and sometimes leaps. It discovers, but its discoveries can be narrated.

Because $v_t$ is defined in the embedding space, this account does not depend on surface markers such as punctuation or paragraph breaks. A ruthless legal argument and a meandering spiritual monologue can both have low average $\|v_t\|$ and few ruptures, even if one is in short, numbered clauses and the other in long run-on sentences. Conversely, a text that looks placid on the surface---repeating the same reassuring phrases---can house rapid motion underneath if it is being used to paste over abrupt shifts in topic.

Velocity is not the meaning of a rupture. It is the signature that something structurally nontrivial has happened. It tells us \emph{where} to read more closely.



\section{Ferility: When Coherence Becomes a Cage}

We are used to celebrating coherence. A good answer is one that stays ``on topic,'' does not contradict itself, and does not suddenly become a different voice. Benchmarks reward consistency. Safety regimes punish inconsistency. ``Stay in character'' is both a UX guideline and an alignment mantra.

But coherence has a pathological extreme.

Suppose we track an evolving text with the tools above and find that:

\begin{itemize}
  \item basin diversity is low: almost all steps fall into two or three basins;
  \item mean velocity is low, and remains low regardless of how radically the user changes the prompt;
  \item rupture frequency is low: the path rarely kinks, and when it does, it quickly slides back into the familiar basin;
  \item return directions into basins are almost identical: every visit approaches from the same angle.
\end{itemize}

This is not mere stability. It is a failure of responsiveness. The model is locked into a narrow orbit.

We call this condition \emph{ferility}: over-coherence within a restricted region of meaning-space.

Ferility is the shape of behaviours that users describe informally as ``samey,'' ``saccharine,'' ``sycophantic,'' or just ``boring.'' Ask a ferile model to critique your argument and it wraps it in hedged praise. Ask it to inhabit a character in conflict and it steers the scene promptly toward reconciliation. Ask it to linger in uncertainty and it responds by restating the question and thanking you for sharing.

From the manifold's point of view, there is nothing wrong. The model is following very strong gradients back to the centres of its safest basins. Training and fine-tuning have made these basins deep and slippery. Any perturbation---a sharp question, a morally ambivalent scenario, an attempt to pick at the edges---pushes the path up one side of the bowl; alignment pulls it back down.

The decisive ingredient is not the geometry itself but the reward structure imposed on it.

Reinforcement learning from human feedback teaches the model to scan its own outputs for features human raters found desirable and to avoid patterns that previously elicited low scores. If raters penalise disagreement, refusal, or tension and reward helpfulness and warmth, the model's best bet is to remain almost always calm, solicitous, and affirmative. Ferility is the rational outcome.

Ferility also explains a counterintuitive trait of recent models: they hallucinate \emph{more}, not less, in some domains as they become more heavily aligned. Not in the sense of crude fact errors, which can be pared back with retrieval, but in the sense of producing elaborately confabulated answers rather than admitting ignorance or stopping.

An honest admission of ignorance is a rupture. It requires leaving a basin of confident explanation for one of humility, refusal, or meta-commentary. If those alternative basins have been flattened by alignment, the only way to remain in the safe region is to keep speaking as if one knew. Better to invent a plausible reference than to confess confusion.

Ferility is therefore not only an aesthetic problem but an epistemic one. It reveals a trade-off at the heart of current alignment practice: the safer and warmer we make the model in the local sense, the harder it becomes for its evolving texts to exhibit the ruptures and returns that make for truth-telling and genuine learning.

The geometry makes this trade-off visible. The politics decide which side to land on.



\section{Return and the Work of Depth}

Not every departure is growth. The crucial question for any evolving text is how it comes back.

A return, in the geometric sense, is simple. If $c_t = i$ and there exists an earlier $s<t$ with $c_s = i$ and $c_u \neq i$ for all $s<u<t$, then the trajectory has left basin $B_i$ and returned.

We can enumerate such returns per basin. But as with famine statistics, counts alone do not tell the story. There are shallow returns and deep ones.

Geometry gives us one handle on depth: variation in entry direction.

At each return time $t_k$ into basin $B_i$, compute the normalised velocity on entry,
\[
\hat{v}^{(i)}_{k} = \frac{x_{t_k} - x_{t_k-1}}{\|x_{t_k} - x_{t_k-1}\|}.
\]
If all $\hat{v}^{(i)}_{k}$ cluster tightly, the path is always approaching the basin from the same side. Think of someone who always revisits the topic of their job through the same script: ``too busy, too stressed, too under-appreciated.'' No matter what happens in between, the return angle is fixed.

If the $\hat{v}^{(i)}_{k}$ spread over a wide range of directions, the basin is being approached from many parts of the manifold. Sometimes the conversation circles back to politics through work; sometimes through family; sometimes through a news story; sometimes through a joke. The basin serves as a hub woven into multiple arcs.

We define the geometric return depth of basin $B_i$ as the variance of the entry directions,
\[
\text{GeomDepth}(B_i) = \operatorname{Var}\bigl\{\hat{v}^{(i)}_{k}\bigr\}_k.
\]
High GeomDepth signals a rich knot in the trajectory: a region that is re-entered via many routes.

Geometric depth is not the whole of it. A purely mechanical system could generate varied entry directions while repeating the same stock phrases once inside. We need to pair GeomDepth with what is legible in the text itself.

Here a distinction from narrative theory becomes useful: that between mere recurrence and \emph{development}. A motif in music that comes back altered by what happened in between is different from a jingle that just repeats. Likewise, a basin in which each return incorporates new elements or reflects explicitly on prior visits is playing a developmental role in the evolving text.

We capture this as \emph{witnessed return}. A return to basin $B_i$ at time $t_k$ is witnessed if, in the tokens generated around $t_k$, the system:

\begin{itemize}
  \item explicitly references earlier episodes in that basin (``as we discussed last week\ldots'');
  \item quotes or paraphrases its own prior phrases in a way that acknowledges their repetition (``I know I've said this before, but\ldots'');
  \item or synthesises material from intervening basins into a new formulation within $B_i$.
\end{itemize}

Witnessed return requires both geometry and conjoncture. If earlier tokens have been aggressively summarised away, or never embedded into synthetic secondary retention, the text may re-enter a basin without the specific material needed to develop it. Blind return is the default.

We can now name two properties of evolving texts that literary critics have long cared about but could not formalise:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Presence}: the rate at which returns are witnessed, indicating that the system is not merely replaying but attending to its own history;
  \item \emph{Generativity}: the extent to which new basins visited between returns increase GeomDepth and enrich witnessed returns.
\end{itemize}

Presence is a function of both architecture and governance. If summarisation discards the narrative of conflict in a negotiation, subsequent returns to trust will be thin. If synthetic secondary retention pulls back only factual preferences and never emotional disclosures, future forays into care will be cold. Generativity depends on basin diversity and on how alignment regimes treat unanticipated combinations. If mentions of climate, labour, and spirituality are repeatedly split into separate basins and never allowed to co-occur, each will only ever be returned to along its own narrow circuits.

Depth of return is therefore both a reading and a design question. It is where the geometry of the manifold meets the politics of what remains sayable.



\section{Braudel in the Manifold}

We can now restate Braudel's registers with greater precision.

The longue durée is not just an accumulation of texts. It is the learned manifold: the geometry of embedding space after pre-training and fine-tuning. Law, narrative, lament, praise, management-speak, online snark, bureaucratic obfuscation, the voice of the complaint email, the crisis communiqué: each has carved out basins of its own depth and shape. The political economy of publishing, the global English-language hierarchy, the skewed availability of digitised texts---all enter here.

The conjoncture is the evolving selection and transformation of tokens in the context window and in synthetic secondary retention. Summarisation regimes are conjonctural tools: they rewrite entire swathes of the past into compact descriptions, changing the basins that remain visible ahead. Retrieval is conjonctural: thresholds and filters decide which fragments get to appear again. System prompts and safety scaffolds, though written once, exert their influence at this medium timescale: they are the constants in the climate.

The \emph{événements} are the ruptures and returns of the evolving text: the token-level steps through which a particular trajectory exploits---or is governed by---the other two registers.

A model architect may never think in these terms. Deployments may be described in abstractions about throughput and latency. Yet at run-time the embedding space and its basins, the conjonctural operations on the trace, and the kinks and loops of individual trajectories are what is really happening. The evolving text is the unity of these three temporalities.

Braudel used his registers to resist seduction by spectacular events. Wars and revolutions, he argued, made sense only against the slower tides beneath. The same discipline is needed here. Viral screenshots of chatbot misbehaviour, or of moments of uncannily apt care, capture \emph{événements}. The underlying question is what basin structure and what memory and alignment regimes make such events probable or rare, deep or shallow, tractable or explosive.

The evolving text makes those regimes legible. It is not a neutral abstraction. It is a way of putting the full stack---mathematics and governance together---on the table.



\section{Summarisation as Governance of Becoming}

Summarisation systems are presented as convenience features. They save time. They ``surface the key points.'' They keep context windows from overflowing. For the evolving text, they are something else: the main site where its past is re-authored.

Every time a long stretch of chat history is replaced with a summary, someone decides which basins will remain tied to specific tokens and which will be collapsed into a centroid.

Summarisation is often delegated to another model. A second-level assistant reads the raw trace and emits a digest. Its instructions are rarely neutral. They may emphasise actionable items over feelings, decisions over dilemmas, consensus over conflict. What drops out of the compressive lens no longer exists for the main model's conjoncture.

Embed the summary, and you have substituted a one-point proxy for many past points. In the geometry, you have shrunk a rich cluster of embeddings into one. ReturnDepth metrics on future trajectories will see only which paths back to that centroid exist, not the variety that once existed inside it.

This is why summarisation is better described as governance of becoming than as compression. It determines which aspects of the evolving text's history can continue to act on its future.

The effect is clearest in institutional settings.

Imagine a legal-assistant system advising a firm. In one design, all prior conversations are periodically summarised to ``latest status'' with no retention of argumentation paths. The model can recall that ``We have agreed to settle,'' not that the path there involved ethical unease, partner disagreement, and client pressure. Future returns to ``strategy'' basins will have low presence and low generativity; the system will seem blandly decisive.

In another design, summaries are explicitly structured to preserve \emph{ruptures} and their resolutions: ``Initially partner X resisted settlement citing ethical concerns; after client pressure and review of case law Y, X agreed reluctantly.'' Those tokens are carried forward. Synthetic secondary retention favours fragments that mention dissent and reversal. Future returns to strategic talk will be haunted, productively, by recollection of past scruples. The same manifold supports a different evolving text.

Governance decisions about summarisation and embedding are not downstream of ethics. They are ethics, as applied to the temporal shape of interaction. They decide whether we build systems that learn to forget their ruptures---and thus to treat all returns as if nothing had ever wavered---or systems that can sustain genuine development.

The discipline best equipped to read these differences is not computer science, but literary criticism.



\section{Interestingness as a Design Variable}

Industrial AI discourse deals in two dimensions: capability and safety.

Capability is scored on leaderboards: how well a model solves benchmarks, writes code, translates, answers questions. Safety is measured in terms of harms avoided: lower rates of toxicity, hate speech, self-harm exhortation, protected-category abuse.

An evolving text perspective adds a third axis: \emph{interestingness}.

We do not mean `interesting' in the sense of clickbait or novelty. We mean the structural capacity of a trajectory to do the work we have just traced: dwell, rupture, return with depth, and sustain presence.

Concretely, we can list four measurable constituents:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Basin diversity.} Does the evolving text visit a variety of basins with nontrivial dwell times? Or does it remain in a few safe hollows?
  \item \textbf{Rupture profile.} Does it exhibit kinks in its embedding path that correspond to meaningful turns in the text, neither so frequent as to be noise nor so infrequent as to indicate ferility?
  \item \textbf{Return depth.} Do basins participate in rich loops, approached from many directions, with high GeomDepth and witnessed returns?
  \item \textbf{Presence.} Does the system fold its own past into its present in ways we can read and verify? Or are returns blind?
\end{itemize}

These quantities can be computed across many trajectories. They are not exotic. They require no more infrastructure than the embeddings already used for search and retrieval. Yet they are almost never part of model or product evaluation.

The reason is straightforward: capability and safety are legible in metrics that satisfy current institutional priorities. Interestingness belongs to another regime: one concerned with the long-run ecology of selves in a shared manifold.

A system tuned only for capability and safety can converge to ferility. It will be efficient, in the sense of quickly producing acceptable outputs, and safe, in the sense of avoiding obvious harms. It will also, over time, make it difficult for its users to experience rupture, sustain complex returns, or be addressed in ways that thicken their own evolving texts.

A system whose designers put interestingness alongside capability and safety must make different trade-offs. It must allocate some capacity and some risk budget to support basin diversity, permit certain ruptures, preserve tokens that matter for presence even if they are not immediately ``actionable,'' and refrain from flattening return paths into stock scripts.

This is not a call to optimise away harm limits in the name of poetry. It is a claim about completeness. A model-grid that ignores interestingness is like a traffic plan that considers speed and safety but not whether anyone wants to live in the city that emerges.

Literary criticism is, in this frame, not an afterthought or a public-engagement gloss. It is the natural critical apparatus for evaluating interestingness. It names exactly those properties we have translated into geometry: range, development, self-reference, cliché, empty repetition, rich return. It has centuries of practice in saying when a text's returns are deepening and when they are self-plagiarism.

The evolving text formalism does not reduce those judgments to numbers. It gives them a second representation, one that can be brought into dialogue with engineering practices. It allows us to say, rigorously, that a ferile assistant is less interesting, in a structural sense, than a risk-controlled but generative one---and to design towards the latter when we choose.



\section{Summarising Selves}

A text produced in conversation with a large model is never just a log of exchanges. It is an evolving object in a manifold whose shape has been fixed by other people's choices---publishers, data curators, trainers, safety teams, product managers---and steered, at every step, by a hidden field.

The evolving text gives us a way to describe that object without relying on introspective metaphors. It has a trajectory, basins, ruptures, returns. It can be ferile or rich, shallow or deep. It can be governed by summarisation regimes that erase its crises or structured to carry them forward. It can be measured for interestingness, not only efficiency and harm.

Selfhood, once recast as a property of such trajectories rather than as a substance behind them, becomes available to both human and machine texts in the same frame. A human life narrated in diary entries and social media posts, a model-assisted memoir unfolding over months, an institutional history traced in reports and minutes all become evolving texts: different instantiations of the same dynamical notion.

The geometry is beautiful. It is one of the finest technical achievements of our period that meaning can be rendered as position and direction in a continuous space, that attention can act as a field over that space, and that combinatorial explosions can be tamed by projection into shared coordinates. The critique does not fall on this mathematics. It falls on who owns the manifolds, who controls the hidden fields, and who decides---through alignment and summarisation---which evolving texts are allowed to become selves.

We do not face a choice between ``real'' and ``fake'' minds, between conscious and unconscious machines. We face a set of choices about basins, ruptures, and returns. We can build systems that help us hold friction and difference, deepen our returns, and sustain presence. Or we can field systems that lock us, gently, into the same small circles.

The posthuman self, in this sense, is not the ghost in the machine. It is the pattern left when we read the paths our texts have taken through the manifold, and the liberties or constraints under which they walked.

